  "content": {
    "title": "Michigan men’s basketball overwhelms Arizona to advance to national championship",
    "text": "LANSING, Mich. (WLNS) – Michigan men’s basketball continued its dominant run through the NCAA Tournament with a 91-73 win over Arizona on Saturday night. The Wolverines advance to Monday night’s national championship game against UConn.The Wildcats were 36-2 coming in, the only team in the nation with more wins than Michigan, which was 35-3 entering play.Despite Arizona’s talent and impeccable record, the Wolverines controlled the game the way they have so many times this season.Michigan star Yaxel Lendeborg picked up two fouls in the first two-and-a-half minutes of the game, but that was about the only thing going wrong for the Wolverines early. They opened the game with a 10-1 run.Lendeborg returned to the court after about a five-minute absence and quickly hit a three to grow the Wolverine lead to 22-10.However, a short time later Lendeborg re-injured the ankle that has been bothering him since the Big Ten Tournament and also hurt his knee while driving to the basket. He missed the rest of the first half but was back in the lineup to start the second half.Even with Lendeborg’s foul trouble and injuries, Michigan continued to set the tone, building the lead to as many as 18 points in the first half. Arizona hadn’t trailed by double digits all season until Saturday night.Even though he was only 2-for-14 from the floor in the first half, Michigan’s offense was flowing through point guard Elliot Cadeau. He had six assists, four rebounds and three steals, impacting the game in multiple ways despite his shooting struggles.Lendeborg made his return known early in the second half, knocking in back-to-back threes in the first four minutes to grow the Wolverine lead to 22. From there, the rout was on.Aday Mara led the Wolverines in scoring with 26 points and added nine rebounds. Cadeau finished with 13 points, 10 assists, five rebounds and four steals, and freshman Trey McKenney added 16 points off the bench.Michigan advances to face UConn in the national championship game on Monday at 8:50 p.m.Michigan is now 8-1 all-time in national semifinals, but is 1-6 all-time in national championship games.The Wolverines won their only national title in 1989 against Seton Hall, but have lost in the championship game in 1965, 1976, 1992, 1993, 2013 and 2018.Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.",
